```{r setup, include = FALSE}
# This file is knitted by vignettes/precompute.R (it runs DeepIMC in Python and the standard methods for about half
# an hour); the package ships the knitted calibrating-windows.Rmd, so building the package runs no code.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "figures/windows-", dev = "png", dpi = 110,
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, out.width = "100%",
                      cache = TRUE, cache.path = file.path(Sys.getenv("VIGNETTE_CACHE", "cache"), "windows/"))
options(width = 110)
```

This vignette shows how to calibrate a *window* of daily counts, a run of
days from any part of an epidemic, with the five methods of the package:

| Method | `method =` | What it does | Time per window |
|---|---|---|---|
| DeepIMC | `"DeepIMC"` | a trained bidirectional LSTM reads the window once | under a second |
| ABC-SMC | `"ABC_SMC"` | approximate Bayesian computation, sequential Monte Carlo | minutes |
| ABC-MCMC | `"ABC_MCMC"` | approximate Bayesian computation, Markov chain Monte Carlo | minutes |
| Differential evolution | `"DE"` | global optimizer (`DEoptim`) of the squared error | minutes |
| Nelder-Mead | `"NELDERMEAD"` | local optimizer (`optim`) of the squared error | seconds |

All five work for the SEIR and the SIR model of `epiworldR`
(`ModelSEIRCONN` and `ModelSIRCONN`). We simulate epidemics whose parameters
we know, so that we can see how close each method comes. The vignettes
`vignette("real-curves")` and `vignette("interventions")` use real data.

## Setup

```{r packages, cache = FALSE}
library(epiworldRcalibrate)
library(epiworldR)
```

DeepIMC runs its networks in Python (through `reticulate`). The package
declares the Python packages it needs (torch, numpy, pandas, scikit-learn,
joblib), and `reticulate` sets them up the first time DeepIMC is used. To use a
Python you already have, set `RETICULATE_PYTHON` before loading the package:

```{r python, eval = FALSE}
Sys.setenv(RETICULATE_PYTHON = "/path/to/python")   # optional
```

The four standard methods need no Python; differential evolution needs the
package `DEoptim`.

## What a method needs: the counts and the known values

A window is the daily number of new infectious people (new cases, or new
hospital admissions) over 15 to 365 days. Besides the counts, every method is
given the values that are known about the disease and the population:

* `n`: the population (or the *effective* population the counts come from);
* `recov`: the recovery rate, 1 / the infectious period in days;
* `incub`: the latent (incubation) period in days, SEIR only.

The methods then estimate the basic reproduction number R0 and the state of
the epidemic on the first day of the window: the shares of the population
exposed (SEIR), infectious and recovered. For a real disease,
`known_values()` gives the values used in the paper:

```{r known}
known_values("COVID-19", "SEIR")
known_values("COVID-19", "SIR")
```

## A simulated SEIR epidemic

We run the agent-based model once with known parameters: 10,000 people,
R0 = 2.5, an infectious period of 7 days and a latent period of 5 days, and 8
contacts per day (so the transmission probability is 2.5 / 7 / 8).

```{r simulate-seir}
simulate_seir <- function(n, R0, recov, incub, crate, prevalence = 0.002, days = 150, seed = 1) {
  m <- ModelSEIRCONN("simulated", n = n, prevalence = prevalence, contact_rate = crate,
                     transmission_rate = R0 * recov / crate, incubation_days = incub, recovery_rate = recov)
  verbose_off(m)
  run(m, ndays = days, seed = seed)
  tr <- get_hist_transition_matrix(m)
  tr <- tr[tr$state_from == "Exposed" & tr$state_to == "Infected", ]
  new <- tr$counts[match(seq_len(days), tr$date)]
  new[is.na(new)] <- 0
  list(new = new, totals = get_hist_total(m))
}
truth <- list(n = 10000, R0 = 2.5, recov = 1 / 7, incub = 5, crate = 8)
sim <- simulate_seir(truth$n, truth$R0, truth$recov, truth$incub, truth$crate)
```

We calibrate the 60 days from day 31 to day 90, and keep the next 14 days to
see whether the fits carry on correctly. The true state on the first day of
the window is the state at the end of day 30.

```{r window-seir}
days <- 31:90
y <- sim$new[days]
future <- sim$new[91:104]
state_at <- function(totals, day, n, states) {
  vapply(states, function(s) totals$counts[totals$date == day & totals$state == s] / n, 0)
}
true_state <- state_at(sim$totals, 30, truth$n, c("Exposed", "Infected", "Recovered"))
round(true_state, 4)
plot(seq_along(sim$new), sim$new, type = "h", col = "grey60", xlab = "day", ylab = "new infectious per day",
     main = "The simulated epidemic and the window")
lines(days, y, type = "h", col = "#2a78d6", lwd = 2)
legend("topright", c("window to calibrate", "rest of the epidemic"), col = c("#2a78d6", "grey60"), lwd = 2, bty = "n")
```

## DeepIMC

`calibrate_window()` takes the counts, the model, the method and the known
values:

```{r deepimc}
known <- list(n = 10000, recov = 1 / 7, incub = 5)
fit <- calibrate_window(y, model = "SEIR", method = "DeepIMC", known = known)
fit
```

The estimate of R0 comes with a 95% range, from the errors DeepIMC made on
the 500 most similar of 100,000 simulated calibration windows. The window
alone does not tell how R0 splits into the transmission probability `ptran`
and the contact rate `crate` (only their product, R0 = ptran x crate / recov,
shapes the curve), so those two are reported but not reliable on their own.
The last line is the *effective* reproduction number on the first day of the
window: R0 times the share of the population still susceptible.

`plot()` runs the agent-based model 200 times from the estimated state, with
parameters drawn from the fit's uncertainty, and shows the median and 95% band
against the counts; with `ahead` it carries on after the window:

```{r deepimc-plot, fig.height = 4}
plot(fit, ahead = 14, future = future)
```

## The five methods on the same window

Give several methods to get all the fits at once. The standard methods run
the agent-based model thousands of times each, so this takes about half an
hour on one core (DeepIMC takes a fraction of a second).

```{r five-seir}
methods <- c("DeepIMC", "ABC_SMC", "ABC_MCMC", "DE", "NELDERMEAD")
fits <- calibrate_window(y, model = "SEIR", method = methods, known = known)
fits
```

`summary()` returns the same table as a data frame. Here we add the error of
each method's R0 and of its starting state:

```{r compare-seir}
tab <- summary(fits)
tab$R0_error <- abs(tab$R0 - truth$R0)
tab$state_error <- vapply(fits, function(f) 100 * mean(abs(f$estimate[c("exposed_share", "infected_share",
                                                                         "recovered_share")] - true_state)), 0)
tab[, c("method", "R0", "R0_lower", "R0_upper", "R0_error", "state_error", "seconds", "model_runs")]
```

`R0_error` is the absolute error of R0 (the truth is 2.5) and `state_error`
the mean absolute error of the three starting shares, in percentage points.
Nelder-Mead and differential evolution give a single best fit, without a
range for R0.

The bands of all methods, with the 14 days after the window as open points:

```{r five-seir-plot, fig.height = 7}
plot(fits, ahead = 14, future = future, ncol = 3)
```

Each panel gives the coverage of the window (the share of days inside the 95%
band) and its interval score (band width plus a penalty for each count
outside it; lower is better).

## The SIR model

The same works for the SIR model, which needs only `n` and `recov`. This time
the window is the first 45 days of a smaller epidemic of 5,000 people with
R0 = 1.8.

```{r simulate-sir}
simulate_sir <- function(n, R0, recov, crate, prevalence = 0.002, days = 150, seed = 2) {
  m <- ModelSIRCONN("simulated", n = n, prevalence = prevalence, contact_rate = crate,
                    transmission_rate = R0 * recov / crate, recovery_rate = recov)
  verbose_off(m)
  run(m, ndays = days, seed = seed)
  tr <- get_hist_transition_matrix(m)
  tr <- tr[tr$state_from == "Susceptible" & tr$state_to == "Infected", ]
  new <- tr$counts[match(seq_len(days), tr$date)]
  new[is.na(new)] <- 0
  list(new = new, totals = get_hist_total(m))
}
sir <- simulate_sir(n = 5000, R0 = 1.8, recov = 0.15, crate = 3)
y_sir <- sir$new[1:45]
fits_sir <- calibrate_window(y_sir, model = "SIR", method = methods, n = 5000, recov = 0.15)
fits_sir
```

```{r sir-plot, fig.height = 7}
plot(fits_sir, ahead = 21, future = sir$new[46:66], ncol = 3)
```

## Bands and scores

`predict_cases()` gives the bands behind these plots: for each day, the
median and 95% limits of the daily new infectious (`new_*`) and of the number
currently infectious (`infected_*`). By default it runs the agent-based model
(`method = "simulation"`); `method = "renewal"` uses the renewal equation,
about 200 times faster.

```{r predict}
band <- predict_cases(fits$DeepIMC, ahead = 14, n = 500)
head(band, 3)
score_band(band, y)                                        # the window
score_band(band, future, days = length(y) + seq_along(future))   # the 14 days after it
```

`coverage()` and `interval_score()` score any band.

## The contact rate

A curve alone shows R0 but not how it splits into `ptran` and `crate`. The
standard methods therefore fix the contact rate at the middle of the range the
networks were trained on (10 contacts a day for SEIR, 3 for SIR) and report
`ptran = R0 x recov / crate`. If the contact rate is known from other data,
for example a contact survey, give it as a known value; every method then
reports the transmission probability that goes with it:

```{r crate}
calibrate_window(y, model = "SEIR", method = "DeepIMC", known = known, crate = 8)
```

## Changing the settings

The standard methods use the settings of the paper (see
`?calibrate_window`). `settings` changes them, one list per method; for
example more particles for ABC-SMC and fewer generations for differential
evolution:

```{r settings, eval = FALSE}
calibrate_window(y, model = "SEIR", method = c("ABC_SMC", "DE"), known = known,
                 settings = list(ABC_SMC = list(n_particles = 500), DE = list(itermax = 50)))
```

By default the ABC methods compare eleven summary statistics of the observed
and simulated curves (`epidemic_summary()`), each scaled by its spread over
simulations from the prior; `abc_distance = "curve"` compares the whole
curves instead.

## Under the hood

`calibrate_window()` builds a *calibrator* and calls `calibrate()`. The same
calibrators are available directly: `window_calibrator()` for DeepIMC (also
for networks you train yourself with `simulate_window_data()` and
`train_window_calibrator()`) and `window_baselines()` for the standard
methods. `compare_methods()` runs several calibrators on a list of windows
and scores them, and `plot_comparison()` and `plot_estimates()` show the
results.

```{r under-the-hood, eval = FALSE}
deep <- window_calibrator("SEIR")
fit <- calibrate(y, deep, n = 10000, recov = 1 / 7, incub = 5)
standard <- window_baselines("SEIR", methods = c("ABC-SMC", "DE"))
fit_de <- calibrate(y, standard$DE, n = 10000, recov = 1 / 7, incub = 5)
```

